WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.580
Part three.

00:00:01.580 --> 00:00:04.871
Free tools that combine health and environmental data.

00:00:05.442 --> 00:00:07.252
Here's the good news.

00:00:07.252 --> 00:00:12.062
People have already combined a lot of this data and put it online for free.

00:00:12.062 --> 00:00:14.092
You have three options.

00:00:14.092 --> 00:00:15.382
Look it up.

00:00:15.382 --> 00:00:17.732
Download a table someone else combined.

00:00:17.732 --> 00:00:19.273
Or combine it yourself.

00:00:19.823 --> 00:00:22.763
Level one: look it up.

00:00:22.763 --> 00:00:28.333
County Health Rankings and Roadmaps gives every US county its own page.

00:00:28.333 --> 00:00:33.063
Health, income, housing, and the environment appear together,

00:00:33.063 --> 00:00:35.413
with the years the data covers.

00:00:35.413 --> 00:00:38.493
Use it when you want to get to know a place fast.

00:00:38.493 --> 00:00:43.558
To access it, go to County Health Rankings dot org and search for a county.

00:00:44.114 --> 00:00:49.394
Data Commons, from Google, is like a search engine for public statistics.

00:00:49.394 --> 00:00:53.514
Type a place and a topic, and you'll see charts from many agencies,

00:00:53.514 --> 00:00:55.634
with the source listed.

00:00:55.634 --> 00:00:58.073
Use it when you don't know where a number lives.

00:00:58.622 --> 00:01:01.592
Level two: download a combined table.

00:01:01.592 --> 00:01:06.382
The Environmental Justice Index, from the CDC, ranks every census tract,

00:01:06.382 --> 00:01:11.832
which is a small area of about 4,000 people, on 36 environmental, social,

00:01:11.832 --> 00:01:13.772
and health factors.

00:01:13.772 --> 00:01:17.832
Use it when you want health and environment in one file.

00:01:17.832 --> 00:01:22.567
To access it, open the index's explorer map, or its data download page.

00:01:23.122 --> 00:01:28.552
The CDC's Environmental Public Health Tracking Network also puts air, water,

00:01:28.552 --> 00:01:31.002
and heat data beside health data.

00:01:31.002 --> 00:01:34.392
Use it to look at environment and health together.

00:01:34.392 --> 00:01:36.952
To access it, open its Data Explorer.

00:01:37.501 --> 00:01:39.261
One warning.

00:01:39.261 --> 00:01:41.481
Government websites change.

00:01:41.481 --> 00:01:48.151
On February 5, 2025, the Environmental Protection Agency took its main public map

00:01:48.151 --> 00:01:52.951
of environmental risks, called EJScreen, offline.

00:01:52.951 --> 00:01:55.841
An outside group rebuilt a copy.

00:01:55.841 --> 00:01:58.467
So download what you use, and write down the date.

00:01:59.017 --> 00:02:02.007
Level three: combine it yourself.

00:02:02.007 --> 00:02:05.607
All you need is two tables and one column they share,

00:02:05.607 --> 00:02:09.497
usually a county ID number called a FIPS code.

00:02:09.497 --> 00:02:15.027
In Excel or Google Sheets, one formula called XLOOKUP does the work.

00:02:15.027 --> 00:02:19.697
For each county in your first table, it finds the matching row in the second.

00:02:19.697 --> 00:02:21.047
Then check.

00:02:21.047 --> 00:02:23.807
Do you still have the same number of counties?

00:02:23.807 --> 00:02:25.398
Did anything come back not found?

00:02:25.948 --> 00:02:28.918
Here's a trap that catches almost everyone.

00:02:28.918 --> 00:02:33.358
Greene County's FIPS code is 01063.

00:02:33.358 --> 00:02:38.028
Open that file in a spreadsheet and the first zero can vanish.

00:02:38.028 --> 00:02:39.678
Now it matches nothing.

00:02:39.678 --> 00:02:44.798
Counties in seven states have this problem, from Alabama to Connecticut.

00:02:44.798 --> 00:02:47.689
So format that column as text before you paste.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.770
Here's a small combined table for two Alabama counties.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:53.590
Diabetes from one source.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.760
Fine particle pollution from another.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.730
Households with no car from a third.

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:02.770
See the labels: the year, and whether each number comes from a survey

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:04.760
or is calculated by a model.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:08.360
Without them, this is just a pile of numbers.

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:10.080
Notice something else.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:15.720
Greene County has less fine particle pollution by this estimate, and more diabetes.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.950
A combined table shows a pattern.

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:19.810
It doesn't explain it.

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:24.880
Understanding the sewage problem in Lowndes County took lab tests, soil facts,

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:26.496
and residents describing their homes.

00:03:27.046 --> 00:03:29.046
You don't need all of these.

00:03:29.046 --> 00:03:31.306
Pick the one that answers your question.

00:03:31.306 --> 00:03:34.706
The Field Guide lists them all, with links.

00:03:34.706 --> 00:03:36.904
Next: how AI can help.

